course: biology
lesson: 301
title: Owls of the world: barn owls, eagle owls, elf owls and burrowing owls
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: There are about 250 kinds of owl, and they live on every continent except Antarctica. Some are as big as a toddler, some as small as a sparrow, some live in holes in the ground and one wades into rivers for fish.
explain: Picture a barn owl at dusk, floating low over a field. Its pale heart-shaped face works like a satellite dish, gathering the tiny rustle of a vole in the long grass towards its ears.
explain: At the other end of the scale the Eurasian eagle owl, with wings almost two metres across, can catch hares and even young foxes, while the elf owl of the desert weighs about as much as a sparrow and eats moths and scorpions.
explain: Owls are real wild hunters, not the wise teachers or post carriers of stories and films, and each kind fits its own home: the snowy owl the Arctic tundra, the burrowing owl the open grassland, the fish owl the old forests along rivers.
fact: There are about 250 kinds of owl, split into two families: the barn owls and the typical owls; owls live on every continent except Antarctica.
fact: A barn owl's heart-shaped face works like a satellite dish, gathering sound towards its ears; barn owls hunt mostly by ear, and in experiments they caught mice in total darkness.
fact: Barn owls do not hoot; they give a long hissing shriek, and white owls shrieking from church towers at night gave rise to many ghost stories.
fact: The Eurasian eagle owl is one of the biggest owls, with wings about 1.6 to 1.9 metres across, and it can catch hares, ducks and even young foxes; its tall ear tufts are only feathers, not ears.
fact: Blakiston's fish owl of the Russian Far East and Japan is one of the heaviest owls; it grabs salmon with rough, spiky soles and needs old hollow trees by rivers to nest in, so it is rare.
fact: The elf owl of the deserts of the USA and Mexico weighs about 40 grams, about as much as a sparrow, often nests in old woodpecker holes in saguaro cactuses and eats moths, crickets and even scorpions.
fact: The burrowing owl of the Americas nests underground, often in burrows dug by prairie dogs or tortoises, because open grassland has few trees; its chicks can make a hiss that sounds like a rattlesnake.
fact: Burrowing owls spread animal dung around their burrows; the dung attracts dung beetles, which the owls eat, a kind of bait and tool use.
fact: Snowy owls are wild birds of the Arctic tundra that hunt lemmings, often in daylight; after popular films some people bought pet owls and later abandoned them, bird charities reported.
fact: The «twit-twoo» of a British wood is usually two tawny owls, one calling «ke-wick» and another answering «hoo-hoo-oo»; a tawny owl pair may stay in the same wood for life.
fact: Owls have four toes on each foot, and the outer toe can swing forward or back, so two can point forward and two back in an X-shaped grip; the osprey can do the same trick.
exercise: Why is a barn owl's face shaped like a heart-shaped dish? | It gathers sound towards the ears | It keeps the rain out of its eyes | It scares mice out of the grass | It helps it smell its prey
exercise: How does a barn owl mostly find voles hidden in long grass? | By hearing them | By smelling them | By feeling the ground shake | By seeing their body heat
exercise: What sound does a barn owl make? | A long hissing shriek | A soft, gentle «twit-twoo» | A loud honk like a goose | No sound at all, ever, not once
exercise: Why did barn owls start so many ghost stories? | White birds shrieked from church towers at night | They glow a ghostly green in the dark at night | They only live in empty, crumbling old castles | They can make themselves invisible
exercise: About how wide are a Eurasian eagle owl's wings from tip to tip? | About 1.6 to 1.9 metres | About 30 centimetres, like a crow | About 4 metres, like a small plane | About 60 centimetres, like a pigeon
exercise: What are the tall tufts on an eagle owl's head? | Only feathers, not ears | Its real ears, sticking up | Little horns of hard bone | Feelers for finding prey
exercise: How does Blakiston's fish owl hold on to a slippery salmon? | With rough, spiky soles on its feet | With a long sticky tongue, like a frog | With a long hooked tail that wraps around it | With webbed feet, just like a duck has
exercise: Why is Blakiston's fish owl rare? | It needs old hollow trees by rivers to nest in | It only eats one kind of fish a year | It cannot fly at all, so foxes catch it easily | It lives only in zoos
exercise: About how much does an elf owl weigh? | About 40 grams, like a sparrow | About 3 kilograms, like a cat | About 1 gram, like a fly | About 400 grams, like a pigeon
exercise: Where does an elf owl often nest? | In old woodpecker holes in saguaro cactuses | In burrows dug by prairie dogs on the open plains | On top of church towers | In nests of mud under bridges
exercise: Why does the burrowing owl nest underground? | Open grassland has few trees, so a hole is safest | It is afraid of the dark sky | Its eggs must stay cool and wet in the mud underground | It cannot fly high enough to reach trees
exercise: Why do burrowing owls spread dung around their burrow? | It attracts dung beetles for them to eat | It keeps the burrow warm through the cold winter | It stops the burrow from flooding when it rains | It is how they mark their birthday each year
exercise: Where do snowy owls really live and what do they hunt? | On the Arctic tundra, hunting lemmings | In castles, carrying letters to schools | In hot rainforests, eating fruit and berries | In deserts, eating scorpions and spiders
exercise: About how many kinds of owl are there in the world? | About 250 | About 12 | About 5,000 | About 2
exercise: What is the «twit-twoo» in a British wood at night? | Usually two tawny owls calling to each other | One barn owl singing a song | A single owl that can sing with two voices at once | A bat copying an owl
exercise: How can an owl grip a wriggling mouse with two toes forward and two back? | Its outer toe can swing backwards | It has two extra toes for winter | It grips with its beak and toes together | It has only two toes on each foot
test: Count them: 3 owls sit on a branch. How many toes is that altogether? | 24 | 18 | 12 | 30
test: Count them: an eagle owl has two tall tufts on its head. How many real ears does it have? | 2, hidden at the sides of its head | 4, two tufts and two hidden | None, owls hear through their feet | 2, inside the tufts
test: «my mate says the shriek from the church tower is a ghost, swear down» What is the truth? | It is very likely a barn owl, which shrieks instead of hooting | Ghosts really live in church towers | Only bats shriek at night, so it must be a bat in the tower | Owls only hoot, so it must be a ghost
test: In the Harry Potter films a snowy owl carries letters. «can i get one as a pet??» What is the best answer? | No, snowy owls are wild Arctic birds needing huge spaces and whole prey | Yes, they love living in a small cage | Yes, they eat ordinary bird seed | Yes, they really carry letters if they are trained from an egg
test: About how many 40-gram elf owls would weigh as much as one 3-kilogram eagle owl? | About 75 | About 7 | About 750 | About 3
test: A fox comes near a burrowing owl's hole and hears a rattlesnake hiss. What is most likely really happening? | The owl chicks are hissing like a snake | A snake has eaten all the owls and is now living in their burrow | The owl is calling its parents | The fox is hearing its own echo
test: What would happen if all the old hollow trees along a river in the Russian Far East were cut down? | Fish owls would have nowhere big enough to nest | Fish owls would happily nest in cactuses instead | Fish owls would start eating seeds | Nothing, fish owls nest in the water
test: In a cartoon, an owl hoots «twit-twoo» all by itself on a branch. What is more true in a real wood? | It is usually two owls, one calling and one answering | Owls never make any sound | Only baby owls make that call when they are hungry | Owls hoot only in the daytime
test: «lol owls live everywhere, even with the penguins in Antarctica» Is that right? | No, owls live on every continent except Antarctica | Yes, snowy owls live there with the penguins | Yes, all owls go there in winter | No, owls live only in Europe
test: In experiments, a barn owl caught a mouse in a completely dark room. How? | It heard the mouse and pounced on the sound | It saw the mouse with its special night eyes | It smelled the mouse from far away | The mouse ran into it by luck
test: An owl and an osprey both catch slippery prey. What do their feet share? | An outer toe that can swing to the back | Webbed feet for swimming | Little suckers on every toe, like an octopus | Five toes on each foot
exam: Identify the principal sense upon which the barn owl relies in order to locate concealed prey. | Hearing, assisted by a facial disc that directs sound towards the ears | Smell, assisted by enlarged nostrils situated above the beak | Touch, assisted by sensitive feathers upon the feet | Taste, assisted by a forked tongue which samples the air for traces of prey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ear tufts of the Eurasian eagle owl? | They consist solely of feathers and are not organs of hearing | They constitute the external ears of the bird | They are horns of bone employed in combat | They are sensory organs for the detection of heat
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the burrowing owl distributes animal dung around the entrance to its burrow. | The dung attracts dung beetles, which the owl subsequently consumes | The dung insulates the burrow against the cold of winter | The dung conceals the scent of the eggs and chicks from all predators | The dung serves as food for the owl's chicks
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the geographical distribution of owls. | Owls occur upon every continent with the exception of Antarctica | Owls occur exclusively within the continent of Europe | Owls occur upon every continent including Antarctica | Owls occur solely within tropical forests
